**What the code does** (`internal/proxy/shardclient/refined_look_aside_balancer.go:258`):

```go
if math.Float64frombits(metrics.serviceTimeSmooth.Load()) == 0 {
    // seed both smoothed values directly with the fresh sample
} else {
    // EWMA
}
```

It treats a smoothed service time of exactly 0 as "not yet seeded". It then overwrites both the service-time and response-time smoothed values with the raw sample. This skips smoothing entirely whenever the smoothed service time is 0.

**What it is meant to do:** `docs/design-docs/design_docs/20261019-look-aside-balancer-fix-design.md`, section "C: EWMA smoothing of cost metrics". The code listing is at lines 134-146 and the "Seeding" bullet is at line 160. The design seeds only when the previous sample has expired or never existed:

```go
if nowTs-metrics.ts.Load() > b.metricExpireInterval { seed } else { ewma }
```

The doc rejects the zero-marker approach in so many words. It says 0 is ServiceTime's normal value for sub-millisecond requests, so seeding on `prev == 0` has two problems:
- **Undamped outliers.** A single 20ms outlier after a run of 0ms samples jumps the smoothed value straight to 20. That defeats the damping fix C exists for.
- **Re-seeding after underflow.** The seeding re-triggers whenever a long run of 0s underflows the float back to 0.

**Practical effects of the difference:**
- **Smoothing bypassed on the target workload.** Nodes that report ServiceTime 0 (the sub-millisecond case) get raw values instead of smoothed ones, whenever the smoothed service time sits at 0.
- **Response time overwritten too.** The check looks only at `serviceTimeSmooth` but reseeds `responseTimeSmooth` as well. A previously smoothed response time is discarded whenever service time is 0.
- **Idle nodes not reseeded.** The expiry-based reseed is missing. An idle node's stale smoothed value is no longer discarded on its first fresh sample, unless the smoothed service time happens to be 0.

The rest of the function matches the design: it returns early on a nil cost, mirrors the sample to the embedded balancer, uses the float64 EWMA, and stores `cost` and `ts`. The fix is to restore the `nowTs-metrics.ts.Load() > b.metricExpireInterval` condition. A brand-new entry has `ts == 0`, so it still seeds.

