**What the code does** (`internal/proxy/shardclient/refined_look_aside_balancer.go:258`):

```go
if math.Float64frombits(metrics.serviceTimeSmooth.Load()) == 0 {
    // seed with the fresh sample
} else {
    // apply the EWMA
}
```

It seeds the smoothed values with the raw sample whenever the smoothed service time is exactly `0`.

**What it is meant to do** is written in `docs/design-docs/design_docs/20261019-look-aside-balancer-fix-design.md`. The code block is at lines 135-146, and the "Seeding" bullet is at line 160. A copy of the doc is also in `.sloppy/notes/01M738319RDZE88W7NA13VK3ED.md`. The design seeds only when the previous sample has expired or never existed:

```go
if nowTs-metrics.ts.Load() > b.metricExpireInterval {
    // seed
} else {
    // EWMA
}
```

A never-seen node has `ts == 0`, so it also takes the seed branch.

The design says outright why a zero-valued check is wrong. A smoothed `0` is a normal value, because `ServiceTime` is 0 for sub-millisecond requests. Seeding on zero causes two problems:

1. **Outliers are undamped.** After a run of 0 ms samples, one 20 ms outlier is stored as 20 instead of 0 + 0.2×20 = 4. This is the single-sample outlier the EWMA is meant to damp.
2. **A float underflow re-triggers seeding.** A long run of 0 samples can underflow the float back to 0, which re-triggers the same seeding.

The zero check also drops the staleness handling. A node that has been idle past `metricExpireInterval` with a non-zero smoothed value no longer gets a fresh seed. Its first new sample is blended into the stale value, which the ts-based rule was meant to prevent.

