**What the code does.** In `internal/proxy/shardclient/refined_look_aside_balancer.go:258`, the first sample is used as the seed whenever the stored smoothed service time is exactly 0:

```go
if math.Float64frombits(metrics.serviceTimeSmooth.Load()) == 0 {
    // seed both smoothed values with the raw sample
} else {
    // apply the EWMA
}
```

**What it was meant to do.** The design doc is `docs/design-docs/design_docs/20261019-look-aside-balancer-fix-design.md`, in the "C: EWMA smoothing" section, around lines 120–155. The same text is in the Refined Look-Aside Balancer note, `sloppy:01M6GSC0WE5894ED5W980J0ZC9`, under "Design Details". It seeds on expiry instead:

```go
// seed the smoothed value with the fresh sample when the previous one has
// expired (or never existed): 0 cannot mark "unseeded" because it is
// ServiceTime's normal value.
if nowTs-metrics.ts.Load() > b.metricExpireInterval {
    // seed from the raw sample
} else {
    // EWMA
}
```

The doc says outright that 0 cannot be the "unseeded" marker, because 0 is a normal `ServiceTime` for sub-millisecond requests. The code uses exactly that marker.

**Consequences.**
1. **Seeding.** A new `RefinedCostMetrics` has `ts == 0`, so the doc's rule seeds on the first sample. The code does too, because the smoothed value is 0.
2. **Stale metrics.** After `metricExpireInterval` with no samples, the doc re-seeds from the fresh sample so old history is dropped. The code never checks expiry here. It blends the new sample into the stale average with alpha, so an old response time keeps influencing the score after the node has been idle. `SelectNode` already treats such metrics as expired (line 207), so the two disagree.
3. **Zero service time.** A node that reports `ServiceTime == 0` (the sub-millisecond case this design targets) looks unseeded on every update. The smoothed service time is overwritten with the raw sample each time, so no smoothing happens for that value.
4. **Mismatched seeding.** The condition checks only the service time, but it re-seeds the response time as well. If service time is 0 and response time is not, the smoothed response time is reset to the raw sample on each update. That defeats the smoothing meant to damp outliers.

The rest of the method matches the design: the nil check, mirroring to the embedded balancer, creating the entry, float64 bit-packing, and storing `cost` and `ts`.

**Fix.** Replace the condition with `nowTs-metrics.ts.Load() > b.metricExpireInterval`. I haven't changed anything.

